              contentEn: 'Fascinatingly, Wittgenstein wrote two major works, and contradicted himself between them. In the first (the Tractatus) he argued there are clear limits to language. In the second (Philosophical Investigations) he retracted this, arguing that language is a flexible social game, not a fixed system.</p><p>What remained constant: <strong>both sought to cure philosophy of its own confusion</strong>. Not to solve the problems, but to show that some of them are not problems at all.',
