            contentEn: 'Kant famously said that <strong>one must never lie, ever</strong>. Even if a murderer comes to your door and asks where your friend is (whom he intends to kill), you must not lie.</p><p>Harries notes that the Categorical Imperative faces difficulties when duties conflict. For example: Suppose you are highly talented and have a duty to develop your skills for the benefit of humanity, but to do so, you must lie once. Two duties, not lying and developing your talents, lead in opposite directions. Kant does not provide a clear tool for resolving such conflicts.</p><p>The Humanity Formula:</p><p>Kant states that all human beings are <strong>"ends in themselves"</strong>, not tools or objects. This is one of the most influential ideas in the history of ethics, from which human rights and human dignity are derived. However, a question Kant did not answer is: What happens when rights conflict? For instance, one person\'s right to life versus another\'s right to freedom?',
