            contentEn: 'Kant said the motive is everything: a right action is done out of duty, not results. Mill said the opposite: <strong>the motive doesn\'t matter at all</strong>. If a wealthy person donated a million dollars to a hospital just to get publicity, the action is still right because it reduced suffering.</p><p>According to Mill, when a person considers what to do, they shouldn\'t ask "What is good for me?", but rather "What is good for everyone?" <strong>Their own happiness is worth exactly as much as the happiness of any other person involved</strong>, no more and no less.</p><p>The Major Critique: What about rights?</p><p>This is the difficult problem of Utilitarianism. Imagine you could save ten people by executing one innocent person. By simple calculation, ten lives are worth more than one. Yet, almost all of us feel this is wrong; that there is something about individual rights that cannot be bought or sold, even for the benefit of the majority.</p><p>Mill tried to answer this by arguing that justice is a special branch of utilitarianism, and that one must weigh the long-term happiness of a society that respects rights. However, many philosophers remained unconvinced.',
