            contentEn: 'Traditional philosophy defined knowledge as <strong>"Justified True Belief"</strong> (JTB). To know something, three conditions are required:</p><p><strong>1. Belief</strong>: You must believe in the thing. If you do not believe it will rain tomorrow, you cannot know it.</p><p><strong>2. Truth</strong>: The thing you believe in must be true. If you believe the earth is flat, this is not knowledge, even if you are absolutely sure of it.</p><p><strong>3. Justification</strong>: There must be a good reason for you to believe. <strong>Guessing correctly by chance is not knowledge, it is luck</strong>.</p><p>Real-life example: "I know it will rain tomorrow", is this knowledge? You believe it. If it indeed rains, then it is true. But what is the justification? If it\'s based on a weather forecast, that\'s close to knowledge. If it\'s just a "gut feeling", it\'s a belief, not knowledge.',
