            contentEn: 'The critique on Plato, <strong>Third Man Argument</strong>:\n\nAristotle asked: if every person has a Form, what connects the specific person to the Form of "person"? You\'d need another Form that connects them. And then a Form connecting all three. And so on to infinity.\n\nThis is the "Third Man Argument", and Plato himself couldn\'t answer it. The additional critique: <strong>the world of Forms sounds beautiful, but what experience can prove that it exists?</strong> It cannot be seen, touched, or measured.\n\nThe critique on Spinoza, <strong>evil and freedom</strong>:\n\nIf everything is one substance acting according to fixed laws, how do we explain evil? Murder, torture, illness, are they also part of the one substance? Spinoza would say: yes. But this feels to many like an answer that erases the distinction between good and evil. Moreover: <strong>if there is no free will, how can one criticize someone for their actions?</strong> How can there be meaning to moral responsibility?\n\nThe critique on Berkeley, <strong>the solution that created a problem</strong>:\n\nBerkeley solved the problem of the disappearing chair through God. But this opens a new question: how do we know that God exists and perceives everything? Berkeley said he proves God\'s existence from the need he has for the solution, but <strong>this is circular</strong>. Hume would say: I cannot know that God exists, just as I cannot know that the external world exists.',
